WEBVTT

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:20.400
<v Ira Glass>Back in the '60s, when Stephen was a young surgeon in Boston, a guy came in with abdominal pain, which turned out to be his gallbladder. He needed an operation. But the guy told Stephen this story of how, 12 years before, he'd come into that exact same hospital and had had cancer. It started in his stomach and had spread to his belly. The doctors opened him up.
<v Ira Glass>回到60年代，当斯蒂芬还是波士顿的一名年轻外科医生时，有个男人因腹痛就诊，结果发现是胆囊的问题。他需要做手术。但那人向斯蒂芬讲述了12年前他曾在同一家医院被诊断出患有癌症的经历。癌症从他的胃部开始，蔓延到了腹部。医生们为他剖开了腹部。

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:34.450
<v Dr. Stephen Rosenberg>And the surgeons had seen cancer that had spread into his liver and throughout his abdomen. They could do nothing and they just closed the abdomen and sent him home to die. And yet, here he was 12 years later, seeming quite healthy.
<v Dr. Stephen Rosenberg>外科医生们发现癌症已经扩散到他的肝脏和整个腹腔。他们对此无能为力，只好缝合伤口让他回家等死。然而，12年后，他却站在了这里，看起来相当健康。

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:42.020
<v Ira Glass>Stephen was skeptical. Cancer usually does not just go away with no treatment at all. The guy must have gotten the story wrong in some way.
<v Ira Glass>斯蒂芬对此表示怀疑。癌症通常不会在没有任何治疗的情况下自行消失。那个人一定是在叙述过程中搞错了什么。

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:59.630
<v Dr. Stephen Rosenberg>Then I went back to the patient's chart and I read, in fact, the pathology reports. And I was still skeptical. So then I went back and actually pulled out the slides to be sure in fact he had had the cancer. And we reviewed the slides, and in fact, it was a cancer. And then I made sure it was in fact the same patient. And in fact, it was.
<v Dr. Stephen Rosenberg>于是我查阅了病人的病历，看了病理报告。我依然表示怀疑。所以我回去调出了当时的切片，以确认他是否真的患过癌症。我们复查了切片，结果显示确实是癌症。然后我确认了这确实是同一位病人。事实证明，就是他。

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:04.060
<v Ira Glass>And then when you opened him up, were you fully expecting you were going to still find some cancer?
<v Ira Glass>那么当你剖开他的时候，你是否完全预料到还会发现一些癌症？

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:24.410
<v Dr. Stephen Rosenberg>Well, it was 12 years later and I didn't know what to expect. I operated on him, took out his gallbladder, examined the inside of his abdomen, and all of the cancer that had been present 12 years earlier had completely disappeared in the absence of any treatment.
<v Dr. Stephen Rosenberg>那是12年后了，我不知道会发现什么。我为他做了手术，切除了胆囊，检查了他的腹腔内部，结果发现12年前存在的那些癌症在没有任何治疗的情况下完全消失了。

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:37.230
<v Dr. Stephen Rosenberg>It appeared that this patient's body had somehow rejected his cancer. This patient set me on a quest for the next 25 years to try to explain how such a thing could happen.
<v Dr. Stephen Rosenberg>看来这位病人的身体以某种方式排斥了他的癌症。这位病人让我开始了此后25年的探索，试图解释这种事情是如何发生的。

00:01:37.230 --> 00:02:27.840
<v Ira Glass>And in fact, this doctor, Stephen Rosenberg, is now a pioneer in inventing immunotherapies based on that research at The National Cancer Institute, where he's chief of surgery. Now, it's interesting, you can find the story about this early case of his all over the place. It circulates on the internet, it shows up in books about people spontaneously recovering from cancer, and he is not happy about that. He kind of hates it, in fact. Because he says that this story gives people false hope. In his career, he's seen maybe 10,000 cancer patients, and this is the only case like this that he has ever encountered. It is just fantastically rare. And it is so easy, he says, for patients to get their hopes up. How could anybody not get their hopes up? And Dr. Rosenberg has seen lots of patients taken advantage of by people who play on this dream of a spontaneous recovery.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，这位医生，斯蒂芬·罗森伯格，现在是基于该项研究发明免疫疗法的先驱，他在美国国家癌症研究所担任外科主任。有趣的是，你可以在各地找到关于他早期这个病例的故事。它在互联网上流传，出现在关于人们从癌症中自愈的书籍里，而他对此并不高兴。事实上，他有点厌恶这一点。因为他说这个故事给了人们虚假的希望。在他的职业生涯中，他见过大约10,000名癌症患者，这是他遇到的唯一一个这样的病例。这实在是太罕见了。他说，患者很容易产生希望。谁能不产生希望呢？而且罗森伯格医生看到许多病人被那些利用这种自愈梦想的人所利用。

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:53.730
<v Dr. Stephen Rosenberg>People who prey on cancer patients, offering quack treatments, often take advantage of cancer patients who are desperate. And I've seen entire families devastated as their entire resources are devoted to treatments of no value.
<v Dr. Stephen Rosenberg>那些掠夺癌症患者、提供江湖骗术的人，往往会利用走投无路的癌症患者。我见过整个家庭因为将全部资源投入到毫无价值的治疗中而变得一无所有。

00:02:53.730 --> 00:03:26.170
<v Ira Glass>Still, hope is important. So what do you do when people place hope in things that probably are not going to work? Well, lots of times his patients come to him asking about vitamins, and herbs, and visualization, and other treatments that have not been proven by science. And if the treatments aren't actually going to hurt them in any way, he says, sure, do it. Studies have shown that patients who are embracing life, feeling more hopeful, feeling more in control of their situation, do better, respond better to medical treatment of all kinds. There are real health effects to hope itself.
<v Ira Glass>尽管如此，希望依然重要。那么，当人们把希望寄托在那些很可能无效的事情上时，该怎么办呢？很多时候，他的病人会来问他关于维生素、草药、意象疗法以及其他未经科学证实的方法。他说，如果这些治疗方法不会以任何方式伤害他们，那就去做吧。研究表明，拥抱生活、感到更有希望、对自己的处境感到更有掌控力的病人，病情会更好，对各种医疗手段的反应也更好。希望本身就有着真实的健康效应。

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:45.620
<v Ira Glass>And so, today on our radio show, we have two stories of hope, the power of hope, hope in some things that are not very likely, I have to say. We have stories about the great side of that and the maybe less great side of that. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>所以，今天我们的广播节目里有两个关于希望的故事，关于希望的力量，以及我必须说，对一些不太可能的事情抱有希望。我们有关于这一面伟大之处的故事，也有可能不那么伟大之处的故事。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:03:45.620 --> 00:04:09.110
<v Ira Glass>Our program today in two acts. Act One, a son tries to help his mom in a faraway place defy the laws of medical science. Act Two, a daughter tries to help her dad by going to a faraway place to defy the laws of the United States of America. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的节目分为两幕。第一幕，一个儿子试图帮助他在远方的妈妈挑战医学科学定律。第二幕，一个女儿试图通过前往远方来帮助她的父亲挑战美利坚合众国的法律。请继续收听。

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:13.030
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Changing the Channeler.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，改变通灵者。

00:04:13.030 --> 00:05:00.450
<v Ira Glass>You may remember Davy Rothbart. He's been on our show a few times. At one point, he came onto the show to tell the story of his mother, Barbara, who's a suburban mom in Ann Arbor, Michigan, who seems like lots of funny, smart moms that you have met in your life, except that she channels an ancient being named Aaron. Aaron is a Buddhist monk. Aaron gives advice along Buddhist lines to people who come into the house. He showed up in Barbara's life-- I think that's the right way to say that. He showed up in Barbara's life years ago. Barbara is also deaf. She hasn't been able to hear anything for decades. And a while back, she heard about this healer in Brazil, a man who goes by the name Joao de Deus, John of God, who, people said, could make the deaf hear. And she wanted to go down and see if he could help her out.
<v Ira Glass>你可能还记得戴维·罗斯巴特。他上过我们节目几次。有一次，他来节目讲述了他母亲芭芭拉的故事，她是密歇根州安阿伯市的一位郊区妈妈，看起来和你生活中遇到的许多风趣、聪明的妈妈没什么不同，只是她会传导一个名叫亚伦的古老存在。亚伦是一位佛教僧侣。亚伦会为来家里的人提供基于佛教理念的建议。他出现在芭芭拉的生活中——我想这样说比较合适。他是几年前出现在芭芭拉生活里的。芭芭拉还是个聋子。几十年来她一直听不见任何声音。前不久，她听说巴西有一位治疗师，名叫若昂·德·德乌斯（上帝的若昂），据说他能让聋人听见声音。她想去看看他能否帮助她。

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:04.860
<v Ira Glass>Davy is really close to her and he agreed to along.
<v Ira Glass>戴维和她很亲近，他同意陪她一起去。

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:23.270
<v Davy Rothbart>She was very hopeful about it. She wouldn't say, I'm going to come back, I'm going to be hearing. But I could just tell that, in the months leading up to our trip, she was getting more and more excited about it. She started just dropping little things in the conversation, like one night, me and her were talking about-- my little brother has a new album coming out.
<v Davy Rothbart>她非常有希望。她不会说“我要回来，我会听得见”，但我能感觉到，在去旅行前的几个月里，她变得越来越兴奋。她开始在谈话中不经意地提到一些小事，比如有一天晚上，我和她聊天——我弟弟有一张新专辑要发行了。

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:24.390
<v Ira Glass>Your brother has a band?
<v Ira Glass>你弟弟有个乐队？

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:37.620
<v Davy Rothbart>Yeah, he's a musician. So my mom, she'd give me a little, funny smile, and she'd just say, I can't wait to hear it. I got excited, too. I talked to her about this on tape.
<v Davy Rothbart>是啊，他是位音乐家。所以我妈妈会给我一个顽皮的小微笑，然后说：“我等不及要听听看了。”我也变得兴奋起来。我在磁带录音中和她谈过这件事。

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:51.250
<v Barbara>I want to hear Peter singing. I want to hear my mother's voice, voices of the people I love. Dad's voice, which I have heard, of course, but will not remember.
<v Barbara>我想听彼得唱歌。我想听我母亲的声音，听我爱的人的声音。爸爸的声音，我当然听过，但已经记不住了。

00:05:51.250 --> 00:06:06.110
<v Ira Glass>Oh, stop the tape for a second, Davy. We should say, your mom is deaf, but she can talk. And she reads lips, so whenever people hear you on these tapes you're talking really slowly and distinctly so she can read your lips, and you're also signing to her a lot of times, too.
<v Ira Glass>噢，戴维，暂停一下录音。我们应该说明一下，你妈妈是聋子，但她会说话。她会读唇语，所以每当人们在这些磁带里听到你说话时，你说话都非常缓慢且清晰，这样她才能读懂你的唇语，而且很多时候你还在用手语和她交流。

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:10.350
<v Davy Rothbart>Right, to exaggerate the space between words.
<v Davy Rothbart>没错，为了夸大词与词之间的间隔。

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:25.430
<v Barbara>I want to hear some of my favorite music. Back at Thanksgiving, you asked me what songs I remembered, and all of my music is from the '60s. I want to hear what's happened to music in 40 years.
<v Barbara>我想听听我最喜欢的一些音乐。感恩节的时候，你问我记得哪些歌，我所有的音乐都是60年代的。我想听听40年来音乐发生了什么变化。

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:29.210
<v Davy Rothbart>Would you like to hear some 50 Cent?
<v Davy Rothbart>你想听点50 Cent的歌吗？

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:29.900
<v Barbara>Some?
<v Barbara>一些？

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:32.860
<v Davy Rothbart>50 Cent?
<v Davy Rothbart>50 Cent？

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:40.460
<v Barbara>Want to hear some 50 Cent? Do you mean two quarters clinging together?
<v Barbara>想听点50 Cent吗？你是说两枚硬币撞击的声音吗？

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.540
<v Davy Rothbart>No, the rapper 50 Cent.
<v Davy Rothbart>不，说唱歌手50 Cent。

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:50.790
<v Barbara>I've never heard of the rapper 50 Cent. I want to hear everything.
<v Barbara>我从来没听说过说唱歌手50 Cent。我想听所有的东西。

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:56.890
<v Davy Rothbart>Back when my mom could hear, she loved listening to music. Now she still sings sometimes.
<v Davy Rothbart>我妈以前能听见的时候，她特别喜欢听音乐。现在她偶尔还会唱歌。

00:06:56.890 --> 00:06:58.360
<v Ira Glass>She still sings sometimes?
<v Ira Glass>她现在还会唱歌？

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:01.860
<v Davy Rothbart>Yeah, it kind of sounds weird. Well, it sounds interesting.
<v Davy Rothbart>是啊，听起来有点奇怪。嗯，听起来很有意思。

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:03.760
<v Ira Glass>Because she can't hear herself at all, right?
<v Ira Glass>因为她完全听不到自己的声音，对吧？

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:06.390
<v Davy Rothbart>Right, she has 0% hearing.
<v Davy Rothbart>对，她听力完全丧失了。

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:52.560
<v Barbara>A favorite song of mine that I love to hear is "Amazing Grace." Do you know that? I can't sing it on tune. [SINGING] Amazing grace, how sweet the sound, that saved a soul like me. I once was lost, but now I'm found, was blind but now I see. [END SINGING] This song, of course, far predates my deafness, but I learned it just before I lost my hearing.
<v Barbara>我最喜欢的一首歌是《奇异恩典》。你知道这首歌吗？我唱不准调。[歌唱] 奇异恩典，何等甘甜，我罪已得赦免。前我失丧，今被寻回，瞎眼今得看见。[歌唱结束] 当然，这首歌在我失聪很久之前就有了，但我在失去听力前刚学会它。

00:07:52.560 --> 00:08:02.390
<v Davy Rothbart>When you can hear again, you will be able to listen to the tapes of yourself singing.
<v Davy Rothbart>等你又能听见的时候，你就能听听你自己唱歌的录音了。

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:08.270
<v Barbara>I'd say, ahhh.
<v Barbara>我会说，啊。

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:20.840
<v Ira Glass>So Davy, before you guys went to Brazil, what did you think was going to happen? Did you view this as, oh, my mom is going to be disappointed? Was there a part of you where you thought that she might get a miracle cure?
<v Ira Glass>那么戴维，在你们去巴西之前，你认为会发生什么？你当时觉得“哦，我妈会失望的”吗？有没有那么一瞬间你觉得她可能会获得奇迹般的治愈？

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:39.900
<v Davy Rothbart>You know what? I had no idea. Who knows? And I think my mom's excitement got me excited about it, too. So I had heard stories from her friend who had been down there and had seen Joao heal deaf people.
<v Davy Rothbart>你知道吗？我完全不知道。谁知道呢？我觉得我妈的兴奋也让我跟着兴奋起来了。我听过她朋友的故事，她去过那里，亲眼见过若昂治愈聋人。

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.470
<v Ira Glass>Wait, and your mom's friend actually saw this happen with her own eyes?
<v Ira Glass>等等，你妈妈的朋友真的亲眼见到过这件事发生？

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:56.640
<v Davy Rothbart>Yeah. So I was also nervous because sometimes it was sort of heartbreaking because I would see her begin to get so excited about it.
<v Davy Rothbart>是的。所以我也很紧张，因为有时这让人有点心碎，我能看到她开始对此变得如此兴奋。

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:58.900
<v Ira Glass>So Davy, where are we now?
<v Ira Glass>那么戴维，我们现在在哪儿？

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:20.230
<v Davy Rothbart>Now we're down in Brazil. It took us 31 hours to get there from Michigan. It's in a tiny town in the middle of Brazil called Abadiania. It's just, really, like a little village with a few farms and some houses, a little factory where they make bricks. And you know, a lot of sick people wandering around.
<v Davy Rothbart>我们现在在巴西。从密歇根到那儿我们花了31个小时。那是在巴西中部一个叫阿巴迪亚尼亚的小镇。它真的就像一个只有几户农场和一些房子、一家制砖小厂的小村庄。而且，你知道，周围到处都是生病的人。

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:25.190
<v Ira Glass>And this is the sort of place where the town's main industry is healing tourists?
<v Ira Glass>这是一个以接待医疗游客为主要产业的小镇吗？

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:49.600
<v Davy Rothbart>Yeah, it's like a one-company town, a miracle town. People have come here on buses from all over Brazil. They'll drive for 36 hours just to visit for a few hours with Joao and then go home. And everywhere you go, people are telling these miracle stories. There's this guy, Marcel, who owns a little pizza restaurant. He actually came there because his dad had been sick.
<v Davy Rothbart>是的，这就像个一业支撑的城镇，一个奇迹之城。人们从巴西各地坐大巴来到这里。他们开车开上36个小时，只为和若昂见上几个小时，然后回家。无论你走到哪里，人们都在讲述这些奇迹故事。有个叫马塞尔的人，他经营着一家小披萨店。他之所以来这里，是因为他爸爸病了。

00:09:49.600 --> 00:10:00.620
<v Man>I have a friend, Alessandro. He lives in Abadiania, and I show you when he walk around. He had multiple sclerosis. That's the right name?
<v Man>我有个朋友，亚历山德罗。他住在阿巴迪亚尼亚，我带你去看他走路的样子。他患有多发性硬化症。是这个名字吗？

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:01.730
<v Davy Rothbart>Yeah, sclerosis.
<v Davy Rothbart>是的，硬化症。

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:20.440
<v Man>OK, he could not walk, problem to move, even to talk. Only his mother could understand him. He came here today, every day you can see he go into university, walking, he play volleyball with the people around.
<v Man>好，他曾经不能走路，移动都很困难，连话都说不清楚。只有他妈妈能听懂他在说什么。他来到这里，现在你每天都能看到他去上大学，走路，他和周围的人一起打排球。

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:38.750
<v Davy Rothbart>I figured, while I'm going with my mom to help her out, see if she can get her hearing back, I'd try to get healed, too. I have this skin thing, it's called psoriasis. And basically, it's like your skin gets kind of all red and scaly looking. And I have it on my chest and my back and part of my scalp.
<v Davy Rothbart>我想，既然我陪妈妈去帮她看能不能恢复听力，我也顺便试试能不能治好我。我有一种皮肤病，叫银屑病。基本上，就是你的皮肤会变得又红又像鳞片一样。我胸部、背部和部分头皮上都有。

00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:46.710
<v Ira Glass>We should say to people who are trying to picture this that we're not talking about like, you just have dry skin, or red skin, that it's way more serious than that.
<v Ira Glass>我们应该对那些试图想象这种情况的人说明一下，我们不是说你只是有干性皮肤或红皮肤，这要严重得多。

00:10:46.710 --> 00:11:06.180
<v Davy Rothbart>Yeah, there's an arthritis that is associated with psoriasis, which I actually have. And it makes your joints and everything terribly painful. They swell up. I've had times when I couldn't walk. And that's scary. So I thought I would, you know, why not see if Joao could heal me, too.
<v Davy Rothbart>是的，银屑病还伴有关节炎，我确实患有这种病。它让你的关节和其他部位疼痛难忍。它们会肿胀。我有过连路都走不了的时候。那很可怕。所以我想，为什么不试试若昂能不能也治好我呢。

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:15.740
<v Woman>Good morning. On behalf of [? Medio ?] Joao Teixeira de Faria, welcome you all to his spiritual center. All belief systems are welcome here.
<v Woman>早上好。我谨代表若昂·特谢拉·德·法里亚，欢迎各位来到他的心灵中心。这里欢迎所有信仰体系。

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:17.630
<v Ira Glass>So where are we now?
<v Ira Glass>所以我们现在在哪儿？

00:11:17.630 --> 00:11:59.870
<v Davy Rothbart>We're in line at the Casa. The Casa is the place where Joao does his healing. It's like a little healing center, compound. And this is the first time we're going to meet him, and then see if he can cure us. It's just early in the morning. We're in the middle of a crowd of, like, 300 or 400 people. And most of them are Brazilian, but about a quarter of us are foreigners from the US and, really, all over the world. Everyone's dressed in white, head to toe. It's like the Casa dress code. You've got to dress in white. And it's funny, they've got Enya bumping over the PA system.
<v Davy Rothbart>我们在“Casa”排队。“Casa”就是若昂进行治疗的地方。它就像一个小型的治疗中心、复合建筑。这是我们第一次见到他，看看他能不能治愈我们。现在还是清晨。我们处在三四百人的人群中间。他们大多数是巴西人，但大约四分之一是我们这些来自美国以及世界各地的外国人。大家从头到脚都穿着白色的衣服。这是Casa的着装要求。你必须穿白色。很有意思，他们通过公共广播系统播放恩雅的音乐。

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:08.460
<v Davy Rothbart>Basically, you have all these people just waiting out in this grand hall, waiting to go before Joao. It just feels like a gigantic low-cost medical clinic.
<v Davy Rothbart>基本上，所有这些人都在这个大厅里等着，等着去见若昂。感觉就像一个巨大的低成本医疗诊所。

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:16.110
<v Woman>Everybody in this first line, please step back a little bit so that we can form the line. It's about 10 deep right now.
<v Woman>第一排的每个人，请稍微往后退一点，这样我们才能排好队。现在大概有10个人深。

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:18.250
<v Davy Rothbart>There's just sick people everywhere.
<v Davy Rothbart>到处都是生病的人。

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:24.160
<v Ira Glass>So Davy, so I know that your mom hired a translator and a guide for you guys. Is she there with you during this part?
<v Ira Glass>戴维，我知道你妈妈为你们雇了一位翻译和导游。她在这一部分和你们在一起吗？

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:35.080
<v Davy Rothbart>Yeah, she's right there. Her name is Heather. While we were waiting there, she told us how Joao had come to be Joao. It wasn't going through medical school.
<v Davy Rothbart>在，她就在那儿。她叫希瑟。我们等待的时候，她告诉我们若昂是如何成为若昂的。他没有上过医学院。

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:55.200
<v Heather>He has a second grade education. And when he was 16 years old, he was coming out of a town called Campo Grande in Mato Grosso. And he'd just lost his job as a tailor's assistant. And he was hungry and tired and lonely and on his way home.
<v Heather>他只有小学二年级的学历。当他16岁时，他正从马托格罗索州一个叫大坎普的镇子出来。他刚刚失去了裁缝助手的工作。他又饿又累，孤独一人，正在回家的路上。

00:12:55.200 --> 00:13:08.150
<v Davy Rothbart>And basically, while he was there on the road, what happened to Joao was he was visited by the spirit of St. Rita. And she gave him just, like, this unfamiliar address. She told him, go back into town, knock on the door at this house.
<v Davy Rothbart>基本上，当他在路上时，发生在若昂身上的事情是，他被圣丽塔的灵魂拜访了。她给了他一个陌生的地址。她告诉他，回到镇上，敲开那户人家的门。

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:13.460
<v Heather>They said, "Are you Joao?" He said, "I'm Joao, I was told to come here. They said, "We've been waiting for you."
<v Heather>他们问：“你是若昂吗？”他说：“我是若昂，有人让我来这里。”他们说：“我们一直在等你。”

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:29.950
<v Heather>The next thing he remembered is coming out of a faint. And he was very apologetic and said, "Gosh, I must have been so hungry that I fainted. I'm so sorry." And they said, "Oh, no. You incorporated King Solomon and you've just healed over 50 people.
<v Heather>他记得的下一件事是从昏迷中醒来。他非常抱歉地说：“天哪，我一定是因为太饿而晕倒了。我很抱歉。”他们说：“哦，不。你被所罗门王的灵魂附身了，你刚刚治愈了50多人。”

00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:34.050
<v Ira Glass>You've incorporated King Solomon. What does that mean?
<v Ira Glass>你被所罗门王附身了。那是什么意思？

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:56.360
<v Davy Rothbart>Incorporating, it's like channeling, except instead of just channeling the voice of a spirit, basically your whole body becomes completely possessed. So the idea is that there's 37 of the world's greatest healers of all time, and they just kind of take turns entering his body and performing these works of healing. They call these guys the entities.
<v Davy Rothbart>附身，就像通灵一样，只是不仅仅是传导一个灵魂的声音，基本上你的整个身体完全被占有了。所以，理念是世界上有37位历史上最伟大的治疗师，他们轮流进入他的身体，并进行这些治愈工作。他们把这些人称为“实体”。

00:13:56.360 --> 00:14:00.340
<v Ira Glass>And are these healers that we would have heard of? Are these, like, famous people?
<v Ira Glass>这些是我们听说过的治疗师吗？他们像名人吗？

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:31.870
<v Davy Rothbart>Oh, there's a few all stars on this roster. You know, there's St. Ignatius and St. Francis, King Solomon. There's a guy named Dr. Octavio Cruz, he's like the father of Brazilian medicine. Each of these spirits has a different personality. In fact, the music that they play over the loudspeakers there, each one has a separate musical preference. So depending on which entity Joao is channeling, they'll play whatever music they most like.
<v Davy Rothbart>哦，名单上有几位全明星人物。你知道，有圣依纳爵、圣方济各、所罗门王。还有个叫奥塔维奥·克鲁兹医生的人，他就像巴西医学之父。这些灵魂每一个都有不同的个性。事实上，他们通过扬声器播放的音乐，每一个都有各自喜欢的音乐。所以取决于若昂正在传导哪个实体，他们就会播放他们最喜欢的音乐。

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:37.630
<v Ira Glass>OK, so you guys are standing there in this big room with hundreds of people in this line. How long does that last?
<v Ira Glass>好了，你们站在那个大房间里，排着数百人的长队。那持续了多久？

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:46.320
<v Davy Rothbart>About a couple of hours. And then Heather says it's our turn. She gets me and my mom into the right line and kind of sends us through to see Joao.
<v Davy Rothbart>大约几个小时。然后希瑟说轮到我们了。她把我推到正确的队列，我们就这样去见若昂。

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:55.960
<v Heather>Just keep coming through. I'll meet you inside.
<v Heather>继续往前走。我在里面接应你们。

00:14:55.960 --> 00:15:35.720
<v Davy Rothbart>First, we go through a couple of rooms where there's just a bunch of people meditating in chairs with their eyes closed. And then, finally, you can see him just way up at the front of the room. He sits in a big, wooden chair. It's almost like a throne. And he's got a beige shirt on, hospital scrubs. He's about 50 years old maybe, dark, wavy hair, round face, glasses. He's sitting there and he's got, like, a ballpoint pen and just a bunch of little slips of paper on his lap. And as each person goes in front of him for a few seconds, he does a real quick little scribble and just gives them the scrap of paper. It's almost like some old baseball player giving autographs at a baseball card show.
<v Davy Rothbart>首先，我们穿过几个房间，里面有一群人在椅子上闭着眼睛冥想。然后，最后，你可以看到他就在房间的最前面。他坐在一把大木椅上。几乎就像一个王座。他穿着米色的衬衫，手术服。他大约50岁，深色的波浪头发，圆脸，戴着眼镜。他坐在那里，膝盖上放着一支圆珠笔和一叠小纸条。当每个人在他面前停留几秒钟时，他会快速乱画几下，就把那张纸条给他们。简直就像某个棒球老将给棒球卡展上的球迷签名一样。

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:45.820
<v Davy Rothbart>So when we get up to see him, Heather explains my mom's deafness to him and she tells him about my psoriasis to Joao in Portuguese.
<v Davy Rothbart>当我们走近见他时，希瑟向他解释了我妈妈的耳聋情况，并用葡萄牙语告诉了若昂我的银屑病。

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:47.750
<v Heather>[SPEAKING PORTUGUESE]
<v Heather>[葡语对话]

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:51.570
<v Joao De Deus>[SPEAKING PORTUGUESE]
<v Joao De Deus>[葡语对话]

00:15:51.570 --> 00:16:23.830
<v Davy Rothbart>To me, this is like the moment of truth maybe. But Joao barely looks at us. I mean, he just kind of nods and gives us one of those papers with this crazy squiggle on it. And Heather tells us it's our prescription. She says that Joao, he doesn't have to actually examine us because the entities have just been checking us out as we were approaching him in line. And she points me and my mom to a room where everyone's meditating and just says, you know, go over there, sit in current. They call it sitting in current.
<v Davy Rothbart>对我来说，这可能是真相大白的时刻。但若昂几乎没看我们一眼。我的意思是，他只是点点头，给了我们一张纸，上面有那个疯狂的涂鸦。希瑟告诉我们这是我们的药方。她说若昂不需要真的检查我们，因为实体在我们排队靠近他时已经检查过我们了。她指引我和妈妈去一个大家都在冥想的房间，就说，去那儿，坐入“电流”中。他们称之为“坐电流”。

00:16:23.830 --> 00:16:33.660
<v Heather>Go sit in the other current room. You stay here till the end. Hold on to that. That's your medicine. I'll tell you later.
<v Heather>去坐进另一个电流房。待在那儿直到最后。拿好那个。那是你的药。我稍后再告诉你。

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:39.730
<v Ira Glass>Current means like the current, the electrical current that comes from all the entities?
<v Ira Glass>“电流”是指所有实体发出的那种电流，电荷吗？

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:51.820
<v Davy Rothbart>Well, when you sit in current, it's like this, everyone that's there meditating is sort of conducting this powerful, spiritual current. And that gives energy to Joao and the entities while they're doing the healing.
<v Davy Rothbart>嗯，当你坐入电流中时，就像这样，在场的所有冥想者都在传导这种强大的精神电流。这为若昂和实体进行治疗时提供了能量。

00:16:51.820 --> 00:17:10.220
<v Davy Rothbart>So you sit in this current room and you're just supposed to keep your eyes closed, make sure not to cross your arms or your legs because that disrupts the flow of energy. Most people in there are sitting pretty still. A couple people have fallen asleep. There's people that are making this weird, spooky, hissing sound. It sounds like this.
<v Davy Rothbart>所以你坐在电流室里，你只需要保持双眼闭合，确保不要交叉双臂或双腿，因为这会阻碍能量的流动。大多数人在里面坐得很静。几个人睡着了。有人发出一种奇怪、阴森的嘶嘶声。听起来像这样。

00:17:10.220 --> 00:17:16.600
<v Davy Rothbart>[HISSING SOUND]
<v Davy Rothbart>[嘶嘶声]

00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:32.240
<v Davy Rothbart>So after like an hour of that, we all went outside and Heather told us to go the pharmacy window. Behind the counter there, it's just a kid sitting on a little stool, probably, like, 15-year-old kid with a Casa t-shirt.
<v Davy Rothbart>大约一个小时后，我们都出去了，希瑟叫我们去药房窗口。柜台后面坐着一个孩子，坐在一个小板凳上，大概是个穿着Casa T恤的15岁男孩。

00:17:32.240 --> 00:18:00.350
<v Davy Rothbart>So me and my mom, we give this kid our pieces of paper with like Joao's scribble on it. And he really doesn't even look at the paper. He just takes them from us. He reaches down into this cardboard box at his feet and he pulls out some pill bottles and just gives us the pills. They're like these little Joao brand pills. And then I see him give the same pills to the guy behind me, and the person behind that guy. We all get the same exact pill bottles.
<v Davy Rothbart>我和妈妈把那张若昂乱涂乱画的纸条递给这个孩子。他真的连看都没看一眼纸条。他就直接从我们手中拿走。他伸手够向脚下的一个纸箱，取出几个药瓶递给我们。它们就像那些“若昂牌”的小药丸。然后我看见他把同样的药瓶给了我后面的人，以及那个人后面的人。我们都得到了完全一样的药瓶。

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:02.690
<v Heather>It's got a [? jaguar ?] on the back.
<v Heather>上面有个美洲豹。

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:11.740
<v Davy Rothbart>Heather tells us that these pills, they're just a passion flower extract, but the entities spike each capsule with a special energy.
<v Davy Rothbart>希瑟告诉我们，这些药丸只是西番莲提取物，但实体为每一粒胶囊注入了特殊的能量。

00:18:11.740 --> 00:18:24.800
<v Heather>It's a vehicle to carry the energy that is imbued into the herbs for that particular person. Unique for you, so you can't give it your sister, your brother. It is for your condition.
<v Heather>这是一种载体，承载着为特定个人注入草药中的能量。对你是独一无二的，所以你不能把它给你的姐妹或兄弟。它是专门针对你的病症的。

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:33.470
<v Davy Rothbart>So Heather tells us what Joao's treatment plans are for me and my mom. Me and my mom are scheduled for surgery.
<v Davy Rothbart>希瑟告诉我们若昂给我和妈妈定的治疗计划。我和妈妈被安排进行手术。

