<v Barbara>你得去刷牙。为了顺着我，喝这个吧。

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:50.950
<v Davy Rothbart>It's the same thing, except this one is cold.
<v Davy Rothbart>是一样的，只是这一瓶是凉的。

00:26:50.950 --> 00:26:56.800
<v Barbara>This has extra energy in it.
<v Barbara>这个里面有额外的能量。

00:26:56.800 --> 00:27:00.950
<v Davy Rothbart>What extra energy?
<v Davy Rothbart>什么额外的能量？

00:27:00.950 --> 00:27:12.520
<v Barbara>I see the skeptical side of you coming to [UNINTELLIGIBLE], and I think that, for your skin condition, I think it would be healing for you.
<v Barbara>我看见你怀疑的一面露出来了，我觉得对于你的皮肤状况，喝这个会有疗效。

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:25.450
<v Davy Rothbart>I'm having trouble a little bit with the whole program here. Why is this holy water different--?
<v Davy Rothbart>对于这里整套程序我有点问题。为什么这圣水不一样……？

00:27:25.450 --> 00:27:39.360
<v Barbara>The entities have really given their energy into this water. They've raised the frequency vibration. And it's a different vibration of [? frequence. ?] It's a charged water, let's call it.
<v Barbara>实体确实把他们的能量注入了这水里。他们提高了频率振动。这是一种不同的振动频率。这是一种充能水，我们称之为。

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:53.580
<v Davy Rothbart>If I gave you the Pepsi challenge, and put some holy water in one cup and some regular bottled water in the other cup, would you tell the difference?
<v Davy Rothbart>如果我给你做个百事可乐挑战，一个杯子里放圣水，另一个杯子里放普通瓶装水，你能喝出区别吗？

00:27:53.580 --> 00:28:10.130
<v Barbara>I don't know. Over a period of days of drinking one or the other, I would feel the difference. But talking about the whole program here, Davy, this is-- I know you respect me.
<v Barbara>我不知道。经过几天只喝其中一种，我能感觉到区别。但说到这儿的整套方案，戴维，这是……我知道你尊重我。

00:28:10.130 --> 00:28:10.790
<v Davy Rothbart>I do.
<v Davy Rothbart>我尊重。

00:28:10.790 --> 00:28:20.100
<v Barbara>This is consistent with everything that I know and have learned in the past 15 years.
<v Barbara>这和我过去15年来所知道和学到的一切是一致的。

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:26.690
<v Davy Rothbart>This is the problem. My mom's trying to use my belief in channeling to get me to belief in Joao.
<v Davy Rothbart>这就是问题所在。我妈试图用我对通灵的信仰来让我去相信若昂。

00:28:26.690 --> 00:28:52.900
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, just to remind people of how you've talked about this in the past, your mom channels this ancient being, Aaron. And the last time you came on the show and talked about it, you described it this way. You said that, basically you faced this choice as her kid. You could decide that channeling isn't real and your mom's a little crazy. And you don't think she's crazy. She's really wonderful in so many ways. And so you sort of provisionally were thinking, OK, well, maybe this is real.
<v Ira Glass>是的，提醒一下人们你过去是怎么谈论这件事的，你妈妈传导那个古老的存在亚伦。你上次来节目谈到它时，你是这样描述的。你说基本上作为一个孩子，你面临这种选择。你可以决定通灵不是真的，你妈有点疯。但你不觉得她疯。她在很多方面都很棒。所以你某种程度上暂时性地在想，好吧，也许这是真的。

00:28:52.900 --> 00:28:59.100
<v Davy Rothbart>Yeah. And I've seen the work that she does with Aaron. And I've seen how helpful it is to people.
<v Davy Rothbart>是的。我见过她和亚伦所做的工作。我见过这对人们有多大帮助。

00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:00.700
<v Ira Glass>Aaron gives people advice?
<v Ira Glass>亚伦会给人们建议？

00:29:00.700 --> 00:29:14.960
<v Davy Rothbart>Yeah, so I've seen the good that comes out of that. But now she's upping the stakes. She's saying, if I'm going to keep believing in Aaron and that stuff, I also have to believe in Joao and the entities.
<v Davy Rothbart>是的，我见过由此带来的善事。但现在她提高了赌注。她说，如果我要继续相信亚伦和那些东西，我也必须相信若昂和那些实体。

00:29:14.960 --> 00:29:17.940
<v Ira Glass>So it's like you're at a kind of showdown with her?
<v Ira Glass>所以就像你和她处于某种摊牌状态？

00:29:17.940 --> 00:29:30.380
<v Davy Rothbart>Yeah. And I didn't realize this was going to happen. But it's true, at home it's easy to sort of each believe what we want to believe and just, it never has to come to a head.
<v Davy Rothbart>是的。我没想到会这样。但确实，在家里我们各自相信想相信的东西很容易，它从来不需要达到顶峰。

00:29:30.380 --> 00:29:35.290
<v Ira Glass>So is there any point down there when you thought that you started to believe a little bit?
<v Ira Glass>那么在那儿有没有一点你觉得你开始相信了？

00:29:35.290 --> 00:29:39.190
<v Davy Rothbart>Yeah, actually. There was one day.
<v Davy Rothbart>有，事实上。有一天。

00:29:39.190 --> 00:29:42.840
<v Woman>All those who have surgery, please raise there hand.
<v Woman>所有接受手术的人，请举手。

00:29:42.840 --> 00:29:45.180
<v Ira Glass>So what's going on now?
<v Ira Glass>那么现在发生什么了？

00:29:45.180 --> 00:29:48.430
<v Davy Rothbart>We're at the Casa. We're going to see some physical surgeries.
<v Davy Rothbart>我们在Casa。我们要去看一些物理手术。

00:29:48.430 --> 00:29:50.360
<v Ira Glass>Oh, you mean like real surgeries?
<v Ira Glass>噢，你是说像真的手术？

00:29:50.360 --> 00:29:50.880
<v Davy Rothbart>Yeah, knife.
<v Davy Rothbart>是的，动刀的。

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:51.580
<v Ira Glass>He does those, too?
<v Ira Glass>他也做那些？

00:29:51.580 --> 00:30:02.890
<v Davy Rothbart>Yeah. He cuts into people. Scrapes their eyes. No anesthetic. Joao wouldn't let me record the operations, but he let me stand right there next to him while he did them.
<v Davy Rothbart>是的。他切开人们。刮他们的眼睛。没有麻醉。若昂不让我录制手术过程，但他让我站在他旁边看他做。

00:30:02.890 --> 00:30:15.600
<v Davy Rothbart>I knew this morning that a couple of the people I'd gotten to be friends with, Gregor and Cynthia, they're from Baltimore. Gregor has MS, so he's in a wheelchair. And Cynthia has some ailments, so she wanted to get surgery, too.
<v Davy Rothbart>我知道今天早上我结识的几位朋友，格雷戈和辛西娅，他们来自巴尔的摩。格雷戈患有多发性硬化症，所以他坐轮椅。而辛西娅有一些疾病，所以她也想做手术。

00:30:15.600 --> 00:30:20.850
<v Ira Glass>OK, so how's it go? You go in there with Cynthia and she's in there with him. What's he do?
<v Ira Glass>好了，过程是怎样的？你和辛西娅一起进去，她和他在一起。他做了什么？

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:38.860
<v Davy Rothbart>Well, she's standing there with her eyes closed and he just kind of tilts her head back a little bit. He's got this wicked-looking instrument. They call it a Kelly clamp. It's about seven inches long. It's basically a long, metal skinny scissors with a little cotton ball kind of in the pliers tip of it.
<v Davy Rothbart>嗯，她闭着眼睛站在那儿，他把她的头稍微往后仰一点。他拿着这个看起来很恐怖的器械。他们称之为凯利钳。大约七英寸长。基本上就是长金属细剪刀，尖端有个棉球。

00:30:38.860 --> 00:30:44.900
<v Ira Glass>OK, I can feel where this is going. Let me just warn people, this might get a little graphic. OK, continue Davy.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我能感觉到接下来要发生什么了。让我警告一下大家，这可能会有点重口味。好的，继续戴维。

00:30:44.900 --> 00:31:01.530
<v Davy Rothbart>He tilts her head back. He dips the point of it just kind of up her right nostril. And then he turns around and just slams it all the way in, so just all the way to the handle, straight back into her head.
<v Davy Rothbart>他把她的头往后仰。他把尖端探进她的右鼻孔。然后他转身，直接把它一直塞了进去，直到握柄处，直接扎进她的头里。

00:31:01.530 --> 00:31:02.420
<v Ira Glass>Oh my God.
<v Ira Glass>我的天。

00:31:02.420 --> 00:31:25.500
<v Davy Rothbart>It was horrifying. It was the most upsetting thing I've ever seen. The room kind of started swirling around me. And everything got really silent. There's sort of like a roar fills your ears. And I just kept watching as he started kind of poking and jabbing around in there. It was like if you're trying to scrape in a jar of peanut butter to get the last little bit out.
<v Davy Rothbart>太恐怖了。这是我见过的最令人不安的事情。房间开始在我周围旋转。一切都变得非常寂静。好像有一阵轰鸣声充斥着你的耳朵。我只是盯着看他开始在里面戳弄。就像你在试图刮花生酱瓶子里的最后一点一样。

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:29.250
<v Ira Glass>OK, and so what is she doing? Is she like screaming? Like, what's she doing?
<v Ira Glass>好了，那她当时在做什么？她是在尖叫吗？她表现如何？

00:31:29.250 --> 00:31:37.810
<v Davy Rothbart>She's just standing there, pretty calmly. I mean, she looks maybe a little uncomfortable. But she's very calm.
<v Davy Rothbart>她就站在那儿，很平静。我的意思是，她看起来可能有点不舒服。但她非常平静。

00:31:37.810 --> 00:31:39.270
<v Ira Glass>Then what happens?
<v Ira Glass>然后发生了什么？

00:31:39.270 --> 00:32:26.820
<v Davy Rothbart>Well, then after 30 seconds of that, he pulls the thing out and she kind of just collapses into a chair. And they wheel her off into this sort of infirmary. And that's the last I see of her for a little while. So I go and just sit down in one of these chairs for another hour or two. I'm freaking out. Because like, I just saw something that should not be possible, that there's no way that could happen. What he just did should have killed her and it didn't. I have to say, it was an hour or two like I've never had before. And I was just completely shaken. I was just sitting there and I didn't know what to believe.
<v Davy Rothbart>嗯，30秒后，他把东西拔出来，她就瘫倒在椅子上。他们把她推到了一个类似医务室的地方。那是之后我见到她的最后一面。所以我去坐了一两个小时。我吓坏了。因为我刚刚看到了一些不该可能发生的事，没有任何方法能让它发生。他刚才所做的本该杀了她，但她却没事。我不得不说，那是我经历过的从未有过的几个小时。我整个人都震撼了。我坐在那儿，不知道该相信什么。

00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:45.830
<v Davy Rothbart>After I'd been sitting there for a couple hours, I went outside and I was standing with Gregor, her husband, who's in a wheelchair. And he was anxious about how she was and then she comes kind of skipping out, just happy as can be. Of course, she'd had her eyes closed the whole time.
<v Davy Rothbart>坐了几个小时后，我到外面和格雷戈站在一起，她丈夫，他坐在轮椅上。他很担心她的情况，然后她兴高采烈地蹦了出来，开心极了。当然，她全程都闭着眼睛。

00:32:45.830 --> 00:32:54.100
<v Cynthia>What does the thing actually even look like, the apparatus? Is it big or is it small? I have no idea.
<v Cynthia>那东西实际上长什么样，那个装置？它是大还是小？我毫无头绪。

00:32:54.100 --> 00:32:57.060
<v Davy Rothbart>It's about a seven-inch scissors, like--
<v Davy Rothbart>大约七英寸的剪刀，像……

00:32:57.060 --> 00:33:01.600
<v Cynthia>Scissors? Now I'm getting scared.
<v Cynthia>剪刀？我现在开始害怕了。

00:33:01.600 --> 00:33:31.380
<v Davy Rothbart>I don't know if it's a good thing for me to describe it to you. Honestly, he twisted it back, so it was going like almost straight back into your brain. And then he just jammed it all the way in. I mean, I thought he had killed you. I honestly did.
<v Davy Rothbart>我不知道描述它对我来说是不是好事。老实说，他把它扭转过来，所以它是几乎笔直地插进你的大脑。然后他只是把它一直塞进去。我的意思是，我以为他杀了你。我真的是这么想的。

00:33:31.380 --> 00:33:35.450
<v Ira Glass>And so did you go and tell your mom? Like, what did your mom make of it?
<v Ira Glass>那你有没有去告诉你妈？你妈对这件事怎么看？

00:33:35.450 --> 00:33:38.630
<v Davy Rothbart>She had an explanation for exactly what was happening.
<v Davy Rothbart>对于到底发生了什么，她有一个解释。

00:33:38.630 --> 00:34:03.750
<v Barbara>The frequency of vibration of these tools is raised so high that they simply become energy and light. Material is transmuted literally into light. And then, as the instrument withdraws the tool, he releases that energy thrust into it and it turns back into the metal that it was.
<v Barbara>这些工具的振动频率被提高得如此之高，以至于它们简单地变成了能量和光。物质实际上被转换成了光。然后，当器械撤回工具时，他将那种能量释放进去，它又变回了原来那种金属。

00:34:03.750 --> 00:34:10.040
<v Davy Rothbart>Then I had talked to a couple doctors and it turns out there's a cavity behind the nose and it goes back a long way.
<v Davy Rothbart>后来我问过几个医生，结果发现鼻子后面有个空腔，而且它向后延伸很长。

00:34:10.040 --> 00:34:12.110
<v Ira Glass>Oh, so there's room for the instrument?
<v Ira Glass>噢，所以是有放器械的空间？

00:34:12.110 --> 00:34:30.300
<v Davy Rothbart>Right. And your brain's surround by bone, so there's no way you can harm it or help it, for that matter, by sticking something up your nose. So really, what Joao did, in the end, isn't proof of miracles, but proof of sinus cavities.
<v Davy Rothbart>没错。而且你的大脑被骨头包围，所以往鼻子里塞东西既伤害不了它，也帮不了它。所以事实上，若昂最后所做的并不是奇迹的证明，而是鼻窦空腔的证明。

00:34:30.300 --> 00:34:39.130
<v Alexander>Some day I'm not good, some day I'm very good. It's with ups and downs. That's it.
<v Alexander>有时候我不舒服，有时候我状态非常好。有起有伏。就是这样。

00:34:39.130 --> 00:34:56.860
<v Davy Rothbart>There's this couple, Tatiana and Ed. They were from Holland. And you saw them all the time pushing their son around the Casa in a wheelchair, Alexander. He had muscular dystrophy. And the doctors in Holland had said there was no cure, like nothing that could be done for him. Here's Tatiana.
<v Davy Rothbart>有对夫妇，塔蒂亚娜和埃德。他们来自荷兰。你总是能看到他们推着儿子亚历山大在Casa转悠。他患有肌营养不良症。荷兰的医生说没有治愈方法，什么也做不了。这是塔蒂亚娜。

00:34:56.860 --> 00:35:32.880
<v Tatiana>You know what? The worst in this illness is that you feel helpless. You feel so very helpless. You know that this boy will die little by little every day in front of your eyes, and you can't do anything. It makes you furious. From time to time, you're furious. You're saying, why? Well, I want to do something about that. I can't just watch it happening like that. This is the only, our chance, and the last one.
<v Tatiana>你知道吗，这种病最糟糕的是你感到无助。你感到非常无助。你知道这个男孩每天都会在你眼前一点点死去，而你却无能为力。这让你愤怒。时不时地，你会愤怒。你会说，为什么？我想对此做点什么。我不能就这样看着它发生。这是唯一的，我们的机会，最后一次机会。

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:53.030
<v Davy Rothbart>Tatiana hadn't believed in any of this stuff before Alexander got sick. But her and Ed were willing to try anything. The doctors had told them that the muscles would just deteriorate until, at age 18 or so, your heart just can no longer even beat, and he'd die. This is Alexander. He's 10.
<v Davy Rothbart>亚历山大生病前，塔蒂亚娜从不相信这些东西。但她和埃德愿意尝试任何事。医生告诉他们肌肉会一直退化，直到18岁左右，心脏甚至无法再跳动，他就会死去。这是亚历山大。他10岁。

00:35:53.030 --> 00:36:00.780
<v Alexander>Well, the entities, they told us positive concentration. So we're working on that. We're doing our best.
<v Alexander>嗯，实体告诉我们积极的专注。所以我们正在努力。我们正在尽我们最大的努力。

00:36:00.780 --> 00:36:01.450
<v Davy Rothbart>What's that?
<v Davy Rothbart>那是什么？

00:36:01.450 --> 00:36:02.940
<v Alexander>We're doing our best.
<v Alexander>我们正在尽力。

00:36:02.940 --> 00:36:04.510
<v Davy Rothbart>Have they told you that you will be healed?
<v Davy Rothbart>他们告诉过你会痊愈吗？

00:36:04.510 --> 00:36:16.670
<v Alexander>Yes, they said a couple of times, he will walk. And then he said another time, you will walk very soon. What soon is for the entities, we don't know.
<v Alexander>是的，他们说过几次，他会走路。然后另一次说，你很快就会走路。对实体来说“很快”是什么意思，我们不知道。

00:36:16.670 --> 00:36:39.280
<v Davy Rothbart>To me, when I meet people like this, hoping for a miracle, it's really the only choice you have. It seems completely sane to me. It's the logical choice.
<v Davy Rothbart>对我来说，当我见到这些期待奇迹的人时，这真的是你唯一的选择。这对我来说完全是理性的。这是合乎逻辑的选择。

00:36:39.280 --> 00:37:14.520
<v Davy Rothbart>One night, one of the posadas hired an accordion player and had a little dance. And everyone was there. Maggie, she's blind, and she's dancing with Dennis. Gregor, who uses a walker at home, but he gets up and twirls for a second with Cynthia. Then he topples over again. I mean, everyone who's here, they are so joyful just being together and having hope. And whether or not you believe in the spirits and everything, it just seems like it's good for you. It improves your health to be in a beautiful place.
<v Davy Rothbart>一天晚上，其中一个客栈雇了一个手风琴手，办了个小舞会。大家都在那儿。玛姬，她是盲人，在和丹尼斯跳舞。格雷戈，他在家使用助行器，但他站起来和辛西娅转了一圈。然后他又倒下了。我的意思是，每一个来这里的人，他们聚在一起抱有希望都感到如此快乐。无论你是否相信灵魂和一切，这看起来对你是有好处的。身处美丽的地方能改善你的健康。

00:37:14.520 --> 00:37:23.120
<v Davy Rothbart>There was 25 people in my mom's group. No one was cured, but everyone was glad that they'd been there.
<v Davy Rothbart>我妈的那组人里有25个。没有人被治愈，但每个人都为来过这里感到高兴。

00:37:23.120 --> 00:37:25.780
<v Ira Glass>Everybody, that is, but you?
<v Ira Glass>除了你？

00:37:25.780 --> 00:37:43.670
<v Davy Rothbart>Right. It wasn't quite enough for me. I mean, my mom still's deaf, my skin's still messed up, and Joao told both of us we needed to come back for more visits. And the worst thing was, my mom and I, we were not seeing eye to eye.
<v Davy Rothbart>没错。对我来说还不够。我的意思是，我妈还是耳聋，我的皮肤还是很糟糕，若昂告诉我们两个都需要回来进行更多访问。最糟糕的是，我和我妈没能达成共识。

00:37:43.670 --> 00:38:02.390
<v Barbara>The thing is, can you open your heart to the possibility that this is real, and that in three or four years you could be healed if you do what you need to do? Do you know what I mean?
<v Barbara>问题是，你能敞开心扉去接受这可能是真的，并且如果你做你需要做的事情，三四年后你可能被治愈吗？你知道我的意思吗？

00:38:02.390 --> 00:38:08.880
<v Davy Rothbart>Kind of, but I just think you and I see the world differently.
<v Davy Rothbart>某种程度上，但我只是觉得你我眼中的世界不同。

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:27.270
<v Barbara>We probably do. We're two different people. I used to see the world the way you do and I've learned some things by banging my head against the wall and then stopping because I realized it wasn't getting me anywhere.
<v Barbara>我们可能确实不同。我们是两个不同的人。我曾经像你那样看待世界，我通过以卵击石学到了一些东西，然后停下来，因为我意识到这对我毫无帮助。

00:38:27.270 --> 00:38:50.690
<v Davy Rothbart>I'm not banging my head. I don't think there is a master plan. I don't think there is a reason for everything. I think there are accidents and coincidences.
<v Davy Rothbart>我没有以卵击石。我不认为有什么宏大的蓝图。我不认为万事皆有因。我认为有偶然和巧合。

00:38:50.690 --> 00:39:08.520
<v Barbara>I don't. And I'm not going to argue that one with you. I don't know, Davy. I would hope that you can just say, let's see what happens.
<v Barbara>我不这么想。我不会和你争论这个。我不知道，戴维。我希望你只能说，让我们看看会发生什么。

00:39:08.520 --> 00:39:21.440
<v Davy Rothbart>I don't want to let you down, but I don't think I'm going to come back here unless it's to visit you because you've moved here or something.
<v Davy Rothbart>我不想让你失望，但除非是为了看你，因为你搬到这里了之类的，否则我不觉得我会回来。

00:39:21.440 --> 00:39:38.150
<v Barbara>You don't think you would come back even though he says he can cure you? That's up to you. That's entirely up to you. That's hard for me to understand why.
<v Barbara>你不觉得你会回来，即使他说能治好你？那是你的选择。这完全取决于你。很难理解为什么。

00:39:38.150 --> 00:39:42.870
<v Davy Rothbart>I don't really believe in it.
<v Davy Rothbart>我并不真正相信它。

00:39:42.870 --> 00:39:53.140
<v Davy Rothbart>That was the first time I had ever told her that, and maybe realized it myself. I don't know what that means exactly for me and my mom.
<v Davy Rothbart>这是我第一次告诉她，也许我自己也才意识到。我不知道这对我和我妈意味着什么。

00:39:53.140 --> 00:40:01.080
<v Ira Glass>You mean, for the two of you who are so close to say, we disagree on something that's so fundamental and such a big part of her life?
<v Ira Glass>你是说，对于两个如此亲近的人来说，在某些如此根本、且在她生活中占如此大一部分的事情上产生了分歧？

00:40:01.080 --> 00:40:31.540
<v Davy Rothbart>Right, because it is everything to her. It's been a couple months since we got back, and she says that she's experiencing some changes. And other people from the group, Maggie, who was blind is saying she's beginning to see a little bit. For my mom, it's the same thing. She says she can feel her inner ears. She can feel changes, shifts, vibrations, and she thinks it's the beginning of hearing.
<v Davy Rothbart>没错，因为它对她来说就是一切。我们回来几个月了，她说她正在经历一些变化。小组里的其他人，盲人玛姬说她开始能看到一点了。对我妈来说，也是一样。她说她能感觉到内耳。她能感觉到变化、移动、振动，她认为这是听力恢复的开始。

00:40:31.540 --> 00:40:40.470
<v Barbara>I can feel my voice going up and down. Suddenly, I have some sense of where the notes are. It's completely different.
<v Barbara>我能感觉到我的声音在上下波动。突然，我对音符在哪里有了一些感觉。这完全不同了。

00:40:40.470 --> 00:40:43.780
<v Davy Rothbart>Do you hear the difference?
<v Davy Rothbart>你能听出区别吗？

00:40:43.780 --> 00:41:26.000
<v Barbara>It's like hearing. It's just vibration. I'm hearing it. I've got to get the tunes, the notes. [SINGING] Amazing grace, how sweet the sound, that saved a soul like me. [END SINGING]
<v Barbara>就像听见一样。只是振动。我听到了。我得抓准旋律、音符。[歌唱] 奇异恩典，何等甘甜，我罪已得赦免。[歌唱结束]

00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:35.550
<v Barbara>Am I way off? Am I better than I was before?
<v Barbara>我跑调了吗？我比以前好了吗？

00:41:35.550 --> 00:41:41.940
<v Davy Rothbart>It sounded beautiful to me.
<v Davy Rothbart>在我听来很美。

00:41:41.940 --> 00:41:53.320
<v Ira Glass>Davy Rothbart is about to head out on a 126-city tour for his new book of excerpts from Found magazine, the magazine he created. Details are at foundmagazine.com.
<v Ira Glass>戴维·罗斯巴特即将为他新书《Found》杂志的节选进行126个城市的巡回演讲，那是他创办的杂志。详情请见foundmagazine.com。

00:41:53.320 --> 00:42:29.460
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC -  "AMAZING GRACE" BY DESTINY'S CHILD FEATURING MISSY ELLIOT]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 真命天女组合和米西·埃利奥特演唱的《奇异恩典》]

00:42:29.460 --> 00:43:41.100
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, summoning the mysterious entities known as the Food and Drug Administration. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来的节目，我们将传召那些被称为“食品药品监督管理局”的神秘实体。一会儿回来，来自芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台。

00:43:41.100 --> 00:43:59.810
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life , I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, Miracle Cures. Stories of kids doing whatever they think it might take, doing the impossible to help their parents get cured. We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two is this.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周，我们当然都会选择一个主题，为您带来各种关于该主题的故事。今天的节目是“奇迹治愈”。关于孩子们为了帮助父母治愈而做他们认为可能需要的一切、做不可能的事情的故事。我们进入节目的第二幕。第二幕是这样的。

00:43:59.810 --> 00:44:20.940
<v Ira Glass>Julia Whitty's dad had cancer, and he had used up all the available kinds of treatment. He probably just had a few months left to live. But there was one hope for him, one last drug that might work. And the problem was, a year of this drug would cost him $47,000, which his insurance couldn't cover and which he could not afford.
<v Ira Glass>朱莉娅·威蒂的爸爸得了癌症，他已经用尽了所有可用的治疗方法。他可能只剩下几个月的时间了。但他还有一个希望，一种可能有效的药。问题是，这种药一年要花47,000美元，他的保险无法覆盖，他也付不起。

00:44:20.940 --> 00:44:34.410
<v Ira Glass>So Julia Whitty, she just wrote a magazine article about this experience. She said that she found when she tried to find this drug for him, she found that most of the ways she could get the drug for less were not exactly legal.
<v Ira Glass>所以朱莉娅·威蒂写了一篇杂志文章来记录这次经历。她说，当她试图为他寻找这种药时，她发现大多数能以更低价格买到药的方法都不怎么合法。

00:44:34.410 --> 00:45:00.110
<v Julia Whitty>I absolutely was willing to break the law and I can't imagine too many people who wouldn't be, in all honesty. If you clearly saw there was a source for what you needed, and if you had to break the law a little bit to cross over the line to go there and get it, I think most people would do that for somebody that they loved. So that was where I began to look around and see if there was some other way we could get this drug.
<v Julia Whitty>我绝对愿意触犯法律，老实说，我无法想象有太多人会不愿意。如果你清楚地看到有你所需要的东西的来源，而且如果你必须为了越过界线去买到它而触犯一点法律，我想大多数人都会为他们所爱的人做这件事。所以这就是我开始四处寻找是否还有其他方法可以买到这种药的初衷。

00:45:00.110 --> 00:45:02.900
<v Ira Glass>So you started looking around, what'd you find?
<v Ira Glass>所以你开始四处寻找，你发现了什么？

00:45:02.900 --> 00:45:44.730
<v Julia Whitty>I went on the internet, as most people would in this day and age, I just started surfing around. And I did find-- that's where I first came in contact with people who might have had a loved one who died and had leftover prescription that they would sometimes give away or sell. But obviously, we needed a steady, reliable, long-term dose of this stuff. And I eventually was able to locate a country that manufactured this drug for completely different diseases. And they were selling it for about $1,200 a year, which obviously started to sound really cheap.
<v Julia Whitty>我在互联网上找的，就像在这个时代大多数人会做的那样，我只是开始到处搜索。我确实发现了——那是我第一次接触到可能家里有爱人去世并留有处方药的人，他们有时会赠送或出售。但显然，我们需要稳定、可靠、长期的药物供应。最终，我设法找到了一个制造这种药的国家，但它是用于完全不同的疾病。他们卖的价格大约是一年1,200美元，这显然听起来非常便宜。

00:45:44.730 --> 00:45:47.500
<v Ira Glass>As compared with $47,000 per year?
<v Ira Glass>相比一年47,000美元？

00:45:47.500 --> 00:45:48.270
<v Julia Whitty>Exactly.
<v Julia Whitty>正是。

00:45:48.270 --> 00:45:54.610
<v Ira Glass>So you go, you fly to some foreign country, you get some doctor there, I guess, to prescribe the drug?
<v Ira Glass>所以你飞往某个外国，找那里的医生开处方吗？

00:45:54.610 --> 00:46:00.350
<v Julia Whitty>Right, which is not difficult to do in other countries. It's pretty much not difficult to do in any country if you're a traveler.
<v Julia Whitty>是的，在其他国家这并不难做到。如果你是个旅行者，在任何国家几乎都不难做到。

00:46:00.350 --> 00:46:05.940
<v Ira Glass>Did you have to actually claim that you had a disease or did you just tell the doctor, here's my situation.
<v Ira Glass>你是必须声称自己得了病，还是直接告诉医生你的处境？

00:46:05.940 --> 00:46:14.780
<v Julia Whitty>I told him the truth regarding my father. And he was more than willing. I think he felt good being able to help me.
<v Julia Whitty>我告诉了他关于我父亲的真相。他非常乐意。我想他能帮到我感觉很好。

00:46:14.780 --> 00:46:18.390
<v Ira Glass>And at this point, you're not exactly sure if it's legal to bring these back?
<v Ira Glass>在这一点上，你不完全确定带它们回来是否合法？

00:46:18.390 --> 00:46:19.330
<v Julia Whitty>That's right.
<v Julia Whitty>没错。

00:46:19.330 --> 00:46:21.130
<v Ira Glass>So what do you do?
<v Ira Glass>那你做了什么？

00:46:21.130 --> 00:46:55.080
<v Julia Whitty>Well, I had thought about it in advance, because I travel in my adult life and I've spent a lot of time in third world countries and stuff. And I usually travel with a pretty hefty medical arsenal. You know, I'd put multiple pills in one bottle. So I thought, well, I'm just going to go there. However they come, I'm going to take them out of that packaging and put them in empty prescription bottles that I have with other prescription names on them. And in all my years of traveling, I've never come back into the US and had anybody open my bottles. So I really didn't think I was going to have trouble with this.
<v Julia Whitty>嗯，我提前考虑过了，因为我在成年生活中经常旅行，在第三世界国家待过很长时间之类的。我旅行时通常会带一个相当庞大的医疗储备。你知道，我会把多片药丸放在一个瓶子里。所以我想，嗯，我要去那儿。无论它们怎么包装，我都会把它们拿出来，放进我有的其他处方药名称的空药瓶里。在我多年的旅行中，我回美国时从来没人打开过我的药瓶。所以我真的没觉得会有麻烦。

00:46:55.080 --> 00:46:59.000
<v Ira Glass>And so this is like, dozens of pills or hundreds of pills?
<v Ira Glass>所以这就像是，几十片药丸还是几百片？

00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:09.670
<v Julia Whitty>It was somewhere between two-, three-, or four-month supply, enough to sort of start my father on it, do a set of scans and see whether or not it was working.
<v Julia Whitty>大概是两三个月或四个月的供应量，足以让我父亲开始服用，做一套扫描看看它是否有效。

00:47:09.670 --> 00:47:14.860
<v Ira Glass>So you have hundreds of pills, what was the moment like when you finally were coming back into the country?
<v Ira Glass>所以你有几百片药丸，你最终回到国内的时候那一刻是什么样的？

00:47:14.860 --> 00:47:30.190
<v Julia Whitty>Well, you definitely get that sweaty palms thing going. You wonder, what are they looking for? What expression on your face? You know you're doing something that could be wrong.
<v Julia Whitty>嗯，你绝对会手心出汗。你想，他们在找什么？你的脸上是什么表情？你知道你在做一些可能是错的事。

00:47:30.190 --> 00:47:34.840
<v Ira Glass>And what do we know about how many people are actually going overseas and bringing back drugs?
<v Ira Glass>对于有多少人真的去海外买药带回来，我们知道多少？

00:47:34.840 --> 00:47:43.330
<v Julia Whitty>I know that their estimates right now are that between one and two million Americans are getting drugs one way or another from Canada alone.
<v Julia Whitty>我知道目前的估计是，仅从加拿大，就有一到两百万美国人通过各种方式获取药物。

00:47:43.330 --> 00:47:46.240
<v Ira Glass>And is that legal or is that illegal?
<v Ira Glass>那这到底是合法还是非法的？

00:47:46.240 --> 00:48:00.660
<v Julia Whitty>In my personal experience, it was probably always illegal, but it wasn't enforced. For whatever reason, it began to be enforced around December of 2002. And I was told by the FDA that they were cracking down at that point.
<v Julia Whitty>以我个人的经历，这大概一直是非法的，只是没有强制执行。不管什么原因，大约在2002年12月开始执行了。食品药品管理局告诉我，那时他们正在严厉打击。

00:48:00.660 --> 00:48:03.670
<v Ira Glass>Do you know what the penalty would have been if you'd been caught?
<v Ira Glass>你知道如果你被抓到，惩罚会是什么吗？

00:48:03.670 --> 00:48:29.160
<v Julia Whitty>I don't know, but I remember being so angry at the time that I was perfectly prepared to go to jail. Not that I want to go to jail, but I was just feeling this was an absurd way to have to live. And it was ridiculous, in light of the fact that this drug could be had more inexpensively and why my father couldn't have access to it. I considered it immoral.
<v Julia Whitty>我不知道，但我记得当时非常生气，我已经做好了坐牢的准备。并不是我想坐牢，但我只是觉得这是一种荒谬的生存方式。考虑到这种药本可以更便宜地获得，以及为什么我父亲无法获得它，这太可笑了。我认为这是不道德的。

00:48:29.160 --> 00:48:38.610
<v Ira Glass>Now, at some point you learned that you might be able to simply order the drug from, what was it, an online manufacturer? Like an online pharmacy?
<v Ira Glass>那么，你在某个时候得知可以直接从，是什么来着，在线制造商订购这种药？就像在线药店？

00:48:38.610 --> 00:49:15.620
<v Julia Whitty>Not an online pharmacy. That would have been easy. It was the actual manufacturer in this country overseas. And they claimed they were already doing it for other patients in the United States. And they told me it would just sail through US customs and that did not turn out to be the case. It gets held up in US customs in Cincinnati and I began a long series of conversations with FDA officials, sort of case officers. And they tell me, it's illegal. You can't bring this in.
<v Julia Whitty>不是在线药店。那倒容易。是海外那个国家的实际制造商。他们声称他们已经在为美国的其他患者这样做了。他们告诉我它会直接通过美国海关，但事实证明并非如此。它在辛辛那提的美国海关被扣留了，我开始了一系列与食品药品管理局官员，类似案件负责人的漫长谈话。他们告诉我，这是违法的。你不能带进来。

00:49:15.620 --> 00:49:21.490
<v Ira Glass>And the reason for that law is because they can't vouch for the quality of the drugs? They haven't tested those drugs?
<v Ira Glass>这条法律的原因是因为他们不能保证药物的质量？他们还没测试过这些药物？

00:49:21.490 --> 00:49:29.280
<v Julia Whitty>Exactly right. They haven't tested those drugs. That's what they say. We haven't tested those drugs, we don't know if they'll be good for you or not.
<v Julia Whitty>完全正确。他们还没测试过这些药物。他们就是这么说的。我们还没测试过那些药，我们不知道它们对你是否有好处。

00:49:29.280 --> 00:49:38.240
<v Ira Glass>But in a way, that's a legitimate thing. Like. In a way, you'd want the government to actually do that, to say, we have no idea if this manufacturer's reputable and the drug's OK.
<v Ira Glass>但在某种程度上，这是一件合情合理的事情。比如，你确实希望政府真的做这件事，去说，我们不知道这家制造商是否可靠，药是否没问题。

00:49:38.240 --> 00:49:56.580
<v Julia Whitty>Well, I would argue that I have the right to take that chance myself. We're just simply not allowed to do this. Now, we can bring in foreign-made food and foreign-made clothes and everything else, but we're not allowed to bring in foreign-made drugs because they are untested supposedly.
<v Julia Whitty>嗯，我会说我有权自己去冒这个险。我们只是不被允许这样做。现在，我们可以带进外国制造的食品和衣服以及其他所有东西，但我们不被允许带进外国制造的药物，因为它们被假定为未经测试。

00:49:56.580 --> 00:50:16.070
<v Ira Glass>There's a passage in your article where you say, "I can go to Europe and buy their wine for less, go to Asia and buy their clothes for less. American companies can buy their raw materials for less overseas, or they can move their operations abroad in order to hire cheaper labor or management. But on a mind-boggling reversal of the American principle of supply and demand, I cannot purchase cheaper drugs and bring them home."
<v Ira Glass>在你的文章中有一段话你说，“我可以去欧洲买他们更便宜的葡萄酒，去亚洲买他们更便宜的衣服。美国公司可以在海外买到更便宜的原材料，或者他们可以把业务转移到国外以雇佣更廉价的劳动力或管理人员。但在一个令人震惊的美国供需原则的颠倒中，我不能购买更便宜的药物并带回家。”

00:50:16.070 --> 00:50:31.800
<v Julia Whitty>That's right. And that's the part that, to this day, infuriates me, particularly about the new Medicare drug bill, which has expressly now, completely, clearly forbidden Americans to go overseas and bring back drugs for any reason whatsoever.
<v Julia Whitty>没错。这就是至今仍让我愤怒的地方，特别是关于新的医疗保险药物法案，现在明确规定完全禁止美国人以任何理由去海外带回药物。

00:50:31.800 --> 00:50:35.960
<v Ira Glass>And so the FDA holds this stuff up. How long do they hold it?
<v Ira Glass>所以食品药品管理局扣留了这些东西。他们扣留了多久？

00:50:35.960 --> 00:51:14.010
<v Julia Whitty>They held it for a couple of weeks. And for the first time, we'd cut his dose in half to try and eke it out, as the package wasn't delivered. Then we cut his dose in half again. Eventually it became clear to me that they were not going to give it to us for any reason whatsoever. And in fact, they were going to destroy it. And that's when I turned to my elected officials. I thought, well, maybe they can help me on this end. One senator, Barbara Boxer, was phenomenally helpful. Her office went to work on it. They just dug their teeth in and they had that package sprung in about 24 hours.
<v Julia Whitty>他们扣留了几周。这是第一次，我们把他的剂量减半以求节省，因为包裹没送到。然后我们再次把剂量减半。最终我很清楚他们无论如何都不会把它给我们。事实上，他们打算销毁它。这时我转向了民选官员。我想，也许他们能帮我解决这个问题。一位参议员，芭芭拉·博克瑟，非常给力。她的办公室为此忙活起来。他们死咬住不放，在24小时内就把那个包裹释放了。

00:51:14.010 --> 00:51:17.770
<v Ira Glass>And did you end up having to get the senator's office to intervene for you again?
<v Ira Glass>你最后不得不再次让参议员办公室为你干预吗？

00:51:17.770 --> 00:51:18.810
<v Julia Whitty>Yes, I did.
<v Julia Whitty>是的。

00:51:18.810 --> 00:51:23.090
<v Ira Glass>Like, every time it would come to the border, the same place, same people?
<v Ira Glass>就像，每次它到达边境，同一个地方，同样的人？

00:51:23.090 --> 00:51:41.680
<v Julia Whitty>No, eventually I figured out a method with the FDA guy that they were willing to accept. We had to provide these very elaborate excuses of why my father was using the foreign version versus the American version of this drug. We had to sort of basically manufacture excuses.
<v Julia Whitty>不，最终我找到了一个食品药品管理局的人愿意接受的方法。我们必须提供这些非常复杂的借口，说明为什么我父亲使用外国版而不是美国版的药。我们必须基本上捏造借口。

00:51:41.680 --> 00:51:45.410
<v Ira Glass>Oh, I see. You had to say that the American version didn't work?
<v Ira Glass>噢，我明白了。你不得不说美国版没用？

00:51:45.410 --> 00:52:01.810
<v Julia Whitty>Didn't work as well for my father for the following reasons, which we pretty much made up. It was pretty bogus, but they went for it. And they did that a few times. That system worked, and then it stopped working.
<v Julia Whitty>对我的父亲来说效果没那么好，原因如下，我们基本上是编造的。虽然很假，但他们接受了。他们做了几次。那套系统有效，然后它失效了。

00:52:01.810 --> 00:52:03.610
<v Ira Glass>Why, what happened?
<v Ira Glass>为什么，发生了什么？

00:52:03.610 --> 00:52:27.700
<v Julia Whitty>At the end of 2002, we were bringing in another shipment and playing the game the way we'd learned to play it. And this time just couldn't get it sprung. And that was when I heard that the law, not that the law had changed, but that enforcement of the law had changed. And they were beginning to really crack down now on letting these drugs in.
<v Julia Whitty>2002年底，我们正在运送另一批货，并按照我们学到的规则玩这个游戏。但这回就是无法放行。那时我听说法律——不是法律变了，而是法律的执行变了。他们现在开始真正严厉打击让这些药物进入。

00:52:27.700 --> 00:52:38.640
<v Julia Whitty>The FDA case officer suggested that I go to the US company that manufactured the drug and apply for patient financial assistance.
<v Julia Whitty>食品药品管理局的案件负责人建议我去制造这种药的美国公司申请患者经济援助。

00:52:38.640 --> 00:52:46.580
<v Ira Glass>Now, that's a special program which lets you say, we don't have the money to pay the $47,000, so give us a break, basically, on humanitarian grounds?
<v Ira Glass>那是一个特殊的项目，允许你说，我们没钱支付那47,000美元，所以基本上在人道主义基础上给我们减免？

00:52:46.580 --> 00:53:37.730
<v Julia Whitty>That's right. And apparently, a lot of the pharmaceutical companies have these things, whether people know it or not, for their more expensive drugs. And we had actually applied for this at the very beginning, when my father was first prescribed this drug, and he didn't pass. He wasn't poor enough, they thought, so I decided we'd just lie. And I called the patient financial assistance line. They had an 800 number. And I was trying to sort of suss them out on, like, how poor did you really have to be before they would accept you? And she said, well, I can't tell you that. But she did, I think inadvertently, admit the only thing I really needed to know, which was that they don't actually check anything you say on the application.
<v Julia Whitty>没错。显然，许多制药公司都有这些项目，无论人们是否知道，针对更昂贵的药物。而在我父亲最初被开这种药时，我们就申请过，但他没通过。他们认为他不够穷，所以我决定我们撒个谎。我打了患者经济援助电话。他们有个800号码。我试图打听他们到底需要多穷才会接受？她说，嗯，我不能告诉你。但她确实，我认为是不小心，承认了我唯一真正需要知道的事情，那就是他们实际上并不会检查你申请表上说的任何内容。

00:53:37.730 --> 00:53:39.880
<v Ira Glass>Oh, she said that to you straight out?
<v Ira Glass>噢，她直接那样告诉你的？

00:53:39.880 --> 00:53:50.110
<v Julia Whitty>She told me straight out. I felt like it was something, she went, oh God, I shouldn't have said that. And in my brain, a little bell just went off and I went, yes.
<v Julia Whitty>她直接告诉我的。我感觉就像，她说了之后意识到，噢，上帝，我不该说那话。在我脑子里，一个小铃铛响了，我说，耶。

00:53:50.110 --> 00:54:13.560
<v Ira Glass>Now, a few months after your dad died-- and we should say that he didn't die of the cancer, he ended up dying of heart failure-- Congress passed the Medicare drug bill, which was designed to address exactly this problem. If your dad had survived, what would this bill have done for him, which is supposed to, basically, take these expensive drugs and put them within financial reach of people?
<v Ira Glass>那么，在你爸爸去世后几个月——我们应该说他不是死于癌症，他最终死于心脏衰竭——国会通过了医疗保险药物法案，旨在解决这个问题。如果你爸爸幸存下来，这项法案会为他做什么？它基本上旨在将这些昂贵的药物置于人们的财务承受范围内。

00:54:13.560 --> 00:54:31.530
<v Julia Whitty>Well, I ran the numbers on it and I found that he would have still had to pay over $6,000 a year. And obviously, that's a lot cheaper. It's still not as cheap as the $1,200-a-year version you could get overseas.
<v Julia Whitty>嗯，我计算了数据，发现他仍然需要每年支付超过6,000美元。显然，那便宜多了。它仍然没有你在海外能买到的那种一年1,200美元的便宜。

00:54:31.530 --> 00:54:35.460
<v Ira Glass>So is that good or bad, as far as you're concerned, getting it down to $6,000 to--
<v Ira Glass>所以，从你的角度来看，把它降到6,000美元是好事还是坏事——

00:54:35.460 --> 00:55:03.850
<v Julia Whitty>It's not good enough. It's not good enough, in my opinion, not if the drug companies are going to be able to ask $47,000, in my father's case, for a drug that all the research and development was done more than 40 years ago. If they have the right to do that, then I think we need to have something. If the government's going to become the biggest drug buyer on the planet, then they need to do something about enacting price controls or setting standards that they're willing to pay.
<v Julia Whitty>还不够好。在我看来这还不够好，如果制药公司能够要求47,000美元，以我父亲的案例为例，买一种所有研究和开发都在40多年前就完成的药。如果他们有权这样做，那么我认为我们需要做点什么。如果政府要成为地球上最大的购药者，那么他们就需要对实施价格管制或设定他们愿意支付的标准采取行动。

00:55:03.850 --> 00:55:14.790
<v Ira Glass>The thing you're talking about is actually that, under the bill, the United States government would not be able to negotiate for lower prices. They wouldn't be able to say, we're buying a huge quantity, so give us a price break?
<v Ira Glass>你谈论的实际上是，根据该法案，美国政府将无法谈判更低的价格。他们无法说，我们正在购买大量药物，所以给我们打折？

00:55:14.790 --> 00:55:20.150
<v Julia Whitty>Right. Congress expressly forbade that in the Medicare drug bill.
<v Julia Whitty>没错。国会在医疗保险药物法案中明确禁止了这一点。

00:55:20.150 --> 00:55:25.270
<v Ira Glass>And as you note in your article, when the government buys anything in quantity, they negotiate the price down.
<v Ira Glass>正如你在文章中指出的，当政府大量购买任何东西时，他们会通过谈判压低价格。

00:55:25.270 --> 00:55:29.940
<v Julia Whitty>That's right. That's another case, I think, where the pharmaceutical industry wrote the law.
<v Julia Whitty>没错。我认为这是另一个制药业编写法律的案例。

00:55:29.940 --> 00:55:32.460
<v Ira Glass>And do you blame the drug companies for that?
<v Ira Glass>你把这归咎于制药公司吗？

00:55:32.460 --> 00:55:51.070
<v Julia Whitty>I blame the drug companies at getting through to our elected officials, yes. They're obviously phenomenally powerful, as we can see by the number of lobbyists that they employ: 600, more than one for every member of Congress. There's a personal lobbyist, in effect, for every member of Congress.
<v Julia Whitty>我归咎于制药公司能够通达我们的民选官员，是的。正如我们从他们雇佣的说客人数中所看到的那样，他们显然极其强大：600人，平均每个国会议员不止一个说客。实际上，每位国会议员都有一个私人说客。

00:55:51.070 --> 00:57:00.620
<v Ira Glass>Julia Whitty's story about getting her dad's $47,000 prescription filled is in the issue of Mother Jones . That is on newsstands now, and it's at motherjones.com.
<v Ira Glass>朱莉娅·威蒂关于她父亲获得47,000美元处方药的故事刊登在《琼斯母亲》杂志上。报刊亭现在有售，也可以在motherjones.com看到。

00:57:00.620 --> 00:57:14.400
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Alex Blumberg and myself with Wendy Dorr, Diane Cook, Sarah Koenig, and Jane Golombisky, who is getting married today. Yes, today. We're going to have to call her Jane Feltes from now on. Our warmest wishes to her and to Rick.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我们的节目今天由亚历克斯·布隆伯格和我制作，还有温迪·多尔、戴安·库克、莎拉·柯尼格和简·戈隆比斯基，她今天结婚。没错，就是今天。我们以后得叫她简·费尔特斯了。我们要向她和里克致以最温暖的祝福。

00:57:14.400 --> 00:57:20.270
<v Ira Glass>Our senior producer is Julie Snyder. Elizabeth Meister runs our website. Production help from Todd Bachmann and Kelsey Dilts.
<v Ira Glass>我们的高级制作人是朱莉·斯奈德。伊丽莎白·迈斯特负责我们的网站。托德·巴赫曼和凯尔西·迪尔茨协助制作。

00:57:20.270 --> 00:57:31.290
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[鸣谢]

00:57:31.290 --> 00:57:49.870
<v Ira Glass>Our website, www.thisamericanlife.org, where you can listen to our programs for free. Or, if you care, you can buy CDs of them now. Well, you know, you can download audio of our show at audible.com/thisamericanlife, where they have public radio programs, bestselling books, even the New York Times , all at audible.com.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，www.thisamericanlife.org，您可以免费收听我们的节目。或者，如果您愿意，现在可以购买它们的CD。嗯，你知道，你可以在audible.com/thisamericanlife下载我们节目的音频，那里有公共广播节目，畅销书，甚至《纽约时报》，都在audible.com上。

00:57:49.870 --> 00:57:54.500
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由国际公共广播电台发行。

00:57:54.500 --> 00:58:22.550
<v Ira Glass>[FUNDING CREDITS]
<v Ira Glass>[资助鸣谢]

00:58:22.550 --> 00:58:30.350
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ management oversight by Torey Malatia. He came into the studio this week to watch us do the radio show. His review?
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ管理监督由托里·马拉蒂亚负责。他这周来到演播室看我们做电台节目。他的评价？

00:58:30.350 --> 00:58:34.100
<v Davy Rothbart>It was horrifying. It was the most upsetting thing I've ever seen.
<v Davy Rothbart>太恐怖了。这是我见过的最令人不安的事情。

00:58:34.100 --> 00:58:38.550
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass, back next week with more stories of this American life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯，下周带着更多关于美国生活的故事回来。

00:58:38.550 --> 00:58:55.460
<v Davy Rothbart>The room kind of started swirling around me and everything got really silent. You know, there's sort of, like, a roar fills your ears.
<v Davy Rothbart>房间开始在我周围旋转，一切都变得非常寂静。你知道，有一阵类似轰鸣声充斥着你的耳朵。

00:58:55.460 --> 00:59:00.460
<v Announcer>PRI, Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>国际公共广播电台。
